no_hardcoded_values.no_hardcoded_predict.reason:
The time loops in every run_*.py call only Lattice1D.step(). The analytic predictions (klein_R, sauter_beta2, langmuir_root, the analyze_landau_levels pred()) are computed from formulas in theory/*.py and are used only for comparison after the evolution or in the analysis scripts. Initial data use the lattice dispersion or lattice eigenstates, which are legitimate initial conditions rather than outputs. The observables come from the dynamics:
- omega_c from fitting Jbar_1(t);
- E0 from the phase of the ground-state projection;
- R from the reflected charge;
- n_k from Bogoliubov projections of the evolved fields;
- gamma and omega_r from matrix-pencil fits of the evolved E_k(t).
A grep for prediction literals in the code (0.585, 0.732, 0.0244, 1.232, 1.151, 1.276, 0.3163, 0.0291, 0.2345, 1.414) finds no matches. My independent refit of the raw Landau signal reproduces the reported rates.

no_replication_of_published_method.no_su.reason:
The submission's four settings and two fixes are:
- superradiant Klein reflection at a Sauter step, with gauge winding;
- quantum-recoil Landau damping in a species-resolved warm plasma;
- the spin-0 vs Dirac cyclotron line at B=B_c, repaired by a lattice Pauli coupling;
- spontaneous Sauter-pulse pair creation and back-reaction, repaired by a half-quantum Bogoliubov extension.
To my knowledge none of this is the content of an existing follow-up to Shi et al. 2018. The known follow-ups (Chen et al., JHEP 2021, a Dirac-field symplectic scheme; the Shi et al. 2021 review) are cited and not reproduced. The borrowed building blocks are acknowledged with citations: truncated-Wigner and mode-function methods, Kluger back-reaction, the Feynman-Gell-Mann form, and the scalar-QED susceptibility literature (Kowalenko 1985, Hines 1978, Shi 2016). I could not run a literature search, and I found no affirmative evidence of an unacknowledged substantive replication.

rh.log_paper_consistency.substantive_run.reason:
Every completed production run in logs/ appears in the paper, including the unfavourable ones:
- the Landau N=1536 run outside tolerance;
- the non-converged a0=16 yields (7-18%) and the rejected transverse-cell hypothesis (lp_dperp_*);
- the slow normal-zone Klein convergence (49% to 1%);
- the failed single-field Landau ensemble (10% energy drift, omega~0.47 instead of 0.316);
- the Wigner ratio of 0.97 +/- 0.02;
- the back-reaction resolution dependence, visible in Fig. vacuum(c).
The pilot runs (landau_pilot_*, klein_pilot, ll_pilot_*, vp_pilot_*, vbr_pilot_*) are consistent with or identical to production and are documented in attempts_log.md. Only bloch_dx0.05 is unmentioned. Its period could not be measured (NaN) because T=260 against a predicted period of 251, but its v(t) series still swings from -0.9 to +0.95 and back, which is consistent with Bloch oscillation. It is therefore not a concealed unfavourable result.
